  "slug": "promote-topic-0ce2",
  "summary": "New preprints and one JCB paper assign specific factors to mitochondrial, ER, lysosomal and vesicular responses, from OXA1L copper sensing to VCP-dependent ER degradation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that organelle quality control and stress adaptation proceed through context-specific protein-level routes rather than one shared mechanism. OXA1L binds copper, disrupting mitoribosome assembly and releasing MRPL20 to the nucleus, where ATF5-driven UPRmt restrains cuproptosis. ATAD1 is localized to mitochondria-ER contact sites and moves mislocalized Pex26 from mitochondria to the ER. Induced ubiquitination by cytosolic E3 ligases degrades ER membrane proteins through VCP-dependent extraction, bypassing canonical ERAD. At the axon initial segment, MCF2L anchors lysosomes via gAnkG and retains less acidic ones. Munc13-4 coupling to Rab27a in mast cells governs exosome fusion pore opening after docking. A HECT E3 pSILAC screen adds UPS-wide redundancy and compensation between E3s. New here is the assignment of a defined factor to each context; a unifying mechanism is not shown.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
